course: everyday
lesson: 441
title: «Only one left!»: shops and adverts
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Ava in Manchester saw a toy with a big red sign: «LAST ONE! HURRY!» She begged Mum till she was nearly crying. Mum said «We'll come back next week, our kid» — in Manchester «our kid» means my brother, my sister or my child. A week later there was still a «last one» on the shelf — and five more in the box underneath. «Last one, my foot,» said Mum. The sign wasn't there to tell Ava something; it was there to hurry her so she would buy before she thought.
explain: Adverts are made to sell, not to tell the whole truth. Leo in Ohio watched a toy car on TV zoom up walls and fly off ramps, with screaming happy kids and loud music. On his birthday it crawled across the carpet and fell over. Dad paused the advert and showed him the tiny writing at the end: «Sequences sped up. Ramp sold separately.» Mason bought a game because a YouTuber said it was «the best game ever made, no cap» — «no cap» is young slang for «no lie». It was mid (average at best), and the video said in small letters: «sponsored». The YouTuber had been paid.
explain: Some tricks hide in numbers. A market stall in Cape Town said «BUY 2, GET 1 FREE!» Zara's gran did the sums: one cost R20 (twenty rand, South African money) last week, and three now cost R60 — the same as before. «Eish, free my foot,» she said («eish» is a South African word for surprise or annoyance). A shampoo advert said «97% of women agreed their hair was shinier» — and in tiny writing: «of 34 women asked». A shop window shouted «70% OFF!» in giant letters, and underneath, small: «up to». One ugly shirt was 70% off; everything else was 10%.
explain: Other tricks are about size and big words. A cereal box said «NOW WITH MORE FRUIT!» — Priya counted three raisins in her bowl. A booking app kept telling Ruby's mum «Only 2 rooms left at this price!» for days, on every computer. A sweet shop in Dublin said «World's Best Fudge». Oisin asked who decided. The owner grinned: «I did, sure.» A man at a car boot sale told Theo's dad a phone was «basically new, barely used»; at home it wouldn't charge, and the man was gone the next week. The ice-cream man in Melbourne gave Kai a cone that looked massive — a tiny scoop sitting on top of an empty cone.
explain: The wise moves are simple. If a shop rushes you, wait a day. Read the small words: «up to», «from», «sponsored», «sold separately». Ask «out of how many?» and «who says so?». Do the sum before you believe «free». Test second-hand things before you pay. Most adverts aren't exactly lies — they're the best bits made bigger, with the rest in tiny print.
fact: Signs like «last one», «only today» and «hurry» are often there to rush you, so you buy before you think.
fact: Adverts can use speeded-up film, close-ups and loud music to make a toy look better than it is.
fact: The tiny writing at the end of an advert, such as «sold separately», often tells the most important truth.
fact: When someone is paid to praise a product, it is an advert; look for «ad», «#ad», «sponsored» or «partnership».
fact: «Buy 2, get 1 free» is only a good deal if the total price is really lower than before.
fact: A big percentage of a small group means little; always ask «out of how many?».
fact: Anyone can write «best in the world» on a sign; nobody has to test it.
fact: «Up to 70% off» means only some things have that discount; most may have much less.
fact: Packaging can make things look bigger than they are; the weight on the label tells the truth.
fact: Things bought from strangers, like at a car boot sale, are hard to return, so test them first.
fact: «Now with more» can be true and still tiny, because it does not say how much more.
fact: Real bargains rarely disappear in a minute; waiting a day is usually safe.
fact: «No cap» (young US slang) means «no lie»; «mid» means average or not good; «our kid» (Manchester) means my brother, sister or child; «eish» (South Africa) shows surprise or annoyance; in London «bare» means lots and «peng» means great.
exercise: Why did the shop put «LAST ONE! HURRY!» on the toy? | To rush shoppers into buying before they think | Because it really was the very last toy | Because the law says shops must warn you when stock runs low | To stop people from buying too many toys
exercise: What did Ava and Mum find a week later? | Another «last one» and five more in a box | An empty shelf where the toy had been | A new sign saying the toy was sold out everywhere in town | The same toy at double the price
exercise: How did the TV advert make Leo's car look amazing? | Speeded-up film, a ramp and exciting music | A different car that was much more expensive | Real tests with the same car on carpet | A famous racing driver steering it
exercise: Where did Leo's dad find the truth about the toy car? | In the tiny writing at the end of the advert | On the big picture on the front of the box, next to the price | In the song that played during the advert | In the colour of the car in the shop
exercise: Why did the YouTuber call the game «the best ever made»? | He was paid to say it, so it was an advert | He had played every game ever made | The game company tested him for honesty before he could post | He always tells the truth to his fans
exercise: Which words show that a video is really an advert? | «Sponsored», «#ad» or «partnership» | «Subscribe», «like» or «comment» | «Part one», «part two», «finale» or «episode» | «Live», «new» or «trending»
exercise: Zara's gran paid R60 for three. One cost R20 before. What was really free? | Nothing — three always cost R60 | One whole item worth R20 | Half of one of the three items | The bag she carried them in
exercise: The shampoo advert said 97% agreed. What did the tiny writing show? | Only 34 women were asked | Thousands of women were tested | Doctors had checked every one | It was 97% of all women in the country
exercise: What does «up to 70% off» really mean? | Only some things have 70% off; most have less | Everything in the shop has 70% off | Everything has at least 70% off | You get 70% off if you buy two
exercise: Who decided the Dublin fudge was the «World's Best»? | The shop owner himself | A world fudge competition | A team of food scientists | The customers in a vote
exercise: Why was the «more fruit» cereal box still a trick? | «More» can be true and still tiny | Cereal boxes are not allowed to say fruit | There was no cereal in the box at all | The fruit was really made of plastic
exercise: What was the trick in the booking app's «only 2 rooms left»? | It said it for days, to rush people | It meant the hotel had burned down | It was a mistake by one computer at the hotel | It meant the price would go down
exercise: What should Theo's dad have done before buying the «barely used» phone? | Tested it before paying | Paid more to make sure it works | Bought two, just to be safe | Asked the seller to promise
exercise: How did the ice cream trick Kai? | The cone was big but the scoop was tiny | The ice cream was a different flavour from the picture | The cone was made of paper | The man charged him twice
exercise: Which label tells you how much is really in a packet? | The weight printed on it | The size of the bag | The picture on the front | The colour of the packet
exercise: A shop rushes you with «only today!». What is the wise move? | Wait a day and think | Buy it at once before it goes | Buy two, just to be safe | Ask the shop to promise
test: «mum it says LAST ONE we HAVE to get it rn». What should Mum say? | Signs like that rush you — let's wait and see | You're right, last ones never come back once they're gone | Quick, buy two before they go | Signs in shops can never be wrong
test: Mason: «he said it's the best game ever, no cap». What does «no cap» mean here? | No lie | No hat | No limit | No money
test: Bruv in London: «bare kids got that game, it's peng». Then: «it's mid tbh». What does «mid» mean? | Average, not great | Brilliant, the best | In the middle of the shop | Half price
test: Your mate says «it's on telly so it must be true». Which is right? | Adverts show the best bits and hide the rest | TV adverts are never allowed to trick | Only online adverts trick people | If it's on telly, it's tested
test: What would happen if the toy car advert showed it at real speed, on carpet? | Fewer kids would beg for it | More kids would want it | The car would go faster | The advert would be against the law
test: Zara's gran: «Eish, free my foot.» How is she feeling about the deal? | Annoyed — it isn't really free | Delighted with a great bargain | Confused about her shopping | Worried that she lost her purse
test: A sign says «9 out of 10 kids love it!» Which question checks it best? | Out of how many kids, and who asked? | What colour is the sign? | Is the shop open on Sunday? | How tall is the shopkeeper?
test: In a cartoon, the hero buys a «magic» product from an advert and it works perfectly. What happens more often in real life? | It works much less well than the advert | It works better than the advert | It never arrives at all | The advert pays you back twice
test: Mum in Manchester: «We'll come back next week, our kid.» Who is «our kid»? | Her own child, Ava | The shopkeeper at the till | A stranger in the shop | The toy on the shelf
test: Dad: «sale's on, everything 70% off!» Then he sees the tiny «up to». What does he find? | Most things have a much smaller discount | Everything is free today | Everything is now more expensive | Only the tiny words are on sale
test: Kai: «yeah nah, I'm not buying the big cone again». What does he mean? | No, he won't buy it again | Yes, he'll buy two next time | Maybe, if it's sunny | He wants a bigger cone
exam: Identify the principal purpose of a notice declaring that an item constitutes «the last one» available. | To induce the customer to purchase without deliberation | To inform the customer of an accurate count of the remaining stock | To comply with a statutory requirement | To restrict purchases to one per customer
exam: It is given that one item was previously priced at twenty rand and that three items are now offered at sixty rand under a «buy two, get one free» promotion. Determine the saving obtained. | No saving whatsoever | A saving of twenty rand | A saving of forty rand | A saving of sixty rand
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a favourable review delivered by a person remunerated by the manufacturer? | It constitutes an advertisement rather than an independent judgement | It constitutes the most reliable evidence available, the reviewer having used the product | It constitutes a scientific test of the product | It constitutes a guarantee of the product's quality
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the statement that ninety-seven per cent of thirty-four persons concurred. | The sample is small and the claim carries limited weight | The claim applies to the entire population | The claim has been verified by measurement | The sample size is irrelevant to the claim
